To print a vector of uint8_t in C++, you can use the following code:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
   std::vector<uint8_t> v = {0xFF, 0xAB, 0xCD};

   for (auto i : v) {
      std::cout << std::hex << static_cast<int>(i) << ' ';
   }
   std::cout << std::endl;

   return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
ff ab cd
```

In this example, we create a vector `v` of uint8_t (also known as unsigned char) and initialize it with three values. We then use a `for` loop to iterate over the vector and print each element in hexadecimal format using `std::cout`.

Note that we use `static_cast<int>(i)` to convert each element to an `int` before printing it, as `cout` cannot directly print `uint8_t`. Also, we use `std::hex` to set the stream's output format to hexadecimal.

Here's an example C++ function that converts a vector<uint8_t> into a string of numbers:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <sstream>

std::string vectorToString(const std::vector<uint8_t>& v) {
    std::stringstream ss;
    for (auto i = v.begin(); i != v.end(); ++i) {
        ss << static_cast<int>(*i) << ' ';
    }
    return ss.str();
}

int main() {
    std::vector<uint8_t> v = {0x01, 0x23, 0x45, 0x67};
    std::string s = vectorToString(v);
    std::cout << s << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
1 35 69 103
```

In this function, we take in a const reference to the vector<uint8_t>. We then create a stringstream object `ss` and iterate through the vector, appending each element (converted to an int using static_cast) and a space character to the stringstream. Finally, we return the stringstream's content as a string.

Note that we use static_cast<int> to convert each uint8_t into an int before appending it to the stringstream. Without the static_cast, the compiler will throw an error due to the implicit conversion from uint8_t to char.

